  "attributionNote": "The attribution is not disputed in the scholarship this catalogue follows.",
  "description": "Ibn Tufail's story of a child raised alone on an island who, without language or teachers, reasons his way from the body to the soul and from the soul to the necessary being. It is a thought experiment about whether philosophy can be reached without revelation, and it ends with the philosopher returning to society and finding that its language cannot carry what he has seen.",
  "historicalContext": "Written in Almohad Spain in the twelfth century, when Ibn Tufail was physician and courtier to the Almohad caliph. It was translated into Latin and English in the seventeenth century and influenced European discussions of natural religion; Brönnle's 1904 rendering is the version printed here.",
  "availabilityNote": "Streamed from Project Gutenberg at read time: the whole plain-text edition is fetched and split at the chapter headings it prints. The 1904 translation by Paul Brönnle is public domain in the United States.",
